 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Terracing Artifacts:** Baking density directly into voxel data and using linear blending causes hard saturation "shells" or step terraces when viewed from inside [S1]. This is avoided by storing a smooth 0–1 gradient and applying opacity at render time via emission-absorption blend [S1].
-*   **Resolution Independence:** The `Density` parameter is resolution-independent [S1]. A cloud at $32^3$ resolution looks comparably dense to one at $64^3$ for the same `Density` value [S1].
-*   **Performance:** `Regenerate` is expensive as it re-samples the whole volume on the CPU [S1]. For animation, use a smaller resolution ($32^3$) and update on a timer (e.g., ~100 ms) rather than every frame [S1].
-*   **Lighting:** `UseLighting` should remain `False` for standard volumetric noise [S1]. Lit clouds require a separate "light-marching" feature [S1].
+*   **Terracing Artifacts:** Naive approaches that bake final density/opacity into voxel data and use linear blending produce hard saturation "shells" or step terraces when viewed from inside. This is solved by storing a smooth 0..1 gradient and applying opacity at render time via emission-absorption blend [S1].
+*   **Performance:** `Regenerate` re-samples the whole volume on the CPU and is expensive. For animation, use a smaller resolution (e.g., 32³) and update on a timer (~100 ms) rather than every frame [S1].
+*   **Lighting:** `UseLighting` should remain False for standard volumetric noise. Lit clouds require a separate "light-marching" feature [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Generation scales linearly with the number of voxels $N = W \times H \times D$ [G]. Each voxel requires $O(\text{Octaves})$ noise evaluations [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the 3D texture [G]. The default resolution is $64^3$ voxels, with a performance preset of $32^3$ [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Regenerating the volume on the CPU is the bottleneck [S1]. Updating `Density` is cheap as it only rebuilds the blend function [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Physically based emission-absorption model prevents internal artifacts [S1]. Resolution-independent density [S1]. Easy-to-use presets [S1]. Fly-through capability [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** CPU-bound generation makes high-resolution animation expensive [S1]. No built-in lighting (requires separate feature) [S1]. Grayscale density by default [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Static or slowly drifting clouds, smoke, and fog where physical accuracy and fly-through are required [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** High-speed animation requiring high resolution [S1]. Scenes requiring direct surface lighting on the volume (use light-marching instead) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Generation is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels ($W \times H \times D$). Each voxel requires $O(\text{Octaves})$ noise evaluations. Rendering is standard volumetric ray marching.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H \times D)$ for the 3D texture. Default resolution is 64³, which can be reduced to 32³ for performance [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Generation is CPU-bound. Animation requires careful management of update frequency to avoid stuttering [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Resolution-independent density appearance (a 32³ cloud looks as dense as a 64³ cloud with the same `Density` parameter); no external data files; fly-through capability [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** CPU-bound regeneration makes high-resolution animation expensive; lacks built-in lighting (requires separate feature) [S1].
+*   **When to use:** For static or slowly drifting clouds, smoke, or fog where performance allows for CPU regeneration or lower resolutions are acceptable [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** For high-frequency animated smoke requiring high resolution, or for lit volumetric clouds (use light-marching instead) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-TGorillaVolumetricNoise is part of the Gorilla3D Framework [S1]. It is designed to provide a ready-to-use cloud, smoke, or fog body without requiring external data files or custom shader code [S1]. It is a subclass of `TGorillaVolumetricMesh` [S1].
+TGorillaVolumetricNoise is part of the Gorilla3D framework [S1]. It is designed to provide a "ready-to-use" cloud/smoke/fog body with a handful of properties, abstracting away the need for shader code or data files [S1]. It builds on the principles of [Procedural and implicit volumes](13-procedural-and-implicit-volumes.md) and [Volumetric Mesh](11-producing-volume-data.md) [S1].
 
